+## JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, applies a JSON Merge Patch to a parsed
+JSON document and returns the merged document:
+
+```ts
+applyMergePatch({ a: "b", c: 1 }, { a: "z", c: null }); // { a: "z" }
+```
+
+An object patch is merged member by member into the target (recursively); a `null` member removes the
+member from the target. Any other patch (array, string, number, boolean, `null`) replaces the target
+as a whole, so arrays are replaced, never merged. As the RFC specifies, a target that is not an object
+is treated as `{}` when the patch is an object, and a `null` inside the patch never appears in the
+result. The result is a new value that shares no structure with the target or the patch, neither of
+which is mutated. It never throws; since `null` means "remove", a merge patch cannot set a member to
+`null`.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test` (Node 22.18 or later, which runs TypeScript directly).
